        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 rounded-2xl p-8 text-gray-200">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="mb-4">
            Two to three pounds of washed 30 to 60 mesh silica or boiler slag per gallon, mixed with a paddle and kept moving, spread by squeegee, in the first coat at minimum. That is the whole recipe, and it is what separates a sealer that lasts one winter from one that lasts two or three.
          </p>
          <p class="m-0">
            Leave it out only when you want a glass-smooth top coat on a flat, sound drive, or when the product is a thin acrylic that cannot carry it. On a slope, do not even have the debate.
          </p>
        </div>

